'''Sōma clan''' (相馬氏) was a prominent Japanese clan of {{ill|samurai|ja|武家|12=draft}} and later part of the {{ill|kazoku|ja|華族|12=draft}} peerage system. The clan traces its origins to {{ill|Taira no Masakado|ja|Taira no Masakado|1=Taira no Masakado|12=draft}}'s son, {{ill|Taira no Masakuni|ja|平将国|12=draft}}, although the direct founder was {{ill|Sōma Moritsune|ja|相馬師常|12=draft}}, the second son of {{ill|Chiba Tsunetane|ja|千葉常胤|12=draft}}.

The Sōma clan controlled {{ill|Nakamura Castle|ja|相馬中村城|12=draft}} during the Edo period, ruling over {{ill|Nakamura Domain|ja|相馬中村藩|12=draft}} as a tozama daimyō with an income of 60,000 koku. After the Meiji Restoration, they became part of the kazoku system, holding the title of viscount.

== History ==

=== Early History ===
The Sōma clan originated in {{ill|Shimōsa Province|ja|下総国|12=draft}}, where Moritsune established the clan's influence. The clan gained further prominence under Moritsune's descendant, {{ill|Sōma Shigetane|ja|相馬重胤|12=draft}}, who moved to {{ill|Odaka|ja|小高|12=draft}}, establishing a stronghold in the northern region.

=== Edo Period ===
During the Edo period, the Sōma clan ruled Nakamura Domain and maintained their position as tozama daimyō. They supported local traditions, such as the {{ill|Sōma Nomaoi|ja|相馬野馬追|12=draft}}, an equestrian festival that continues to this day.

=== Meiji Period and Modern Era ===
After the abolition of the han system, the clan joined the kazoku peerage system. The last daimyō, {{ill|Sōma Makototane|ja|相馬誠胤|12=draft}}, became the first viscount. Today, the Sōma family is associated with various cultural and historical activities, including managing local agricultural projects in Fukushima.

